# THIS REPOSITORY'S OWN INSTALLER COMPARES ITS BYTES, so the bytes have to be
# the same bytes on every platform.
#
# `openRepoTools --install` decides between `installed at`, `updated at` and
# `already installed … (unchanged)` with `cmp -s`, and
# `tests/test_openrepotools_command.py` compares an installed copy to this
# checkout's file BYTE FOR BYTE. Git for Windows' installer default is
# `core.autocrlf=true`; a checkout of THIS repository made under it turns every
# `\n` in `openRepoTools`, `park`, `resume` and `status` into `\r\n`, and every one
# of those comparisons is about line endings rather than about content — a
# second `--install` reporting three files `updated at` on a machine where
# nothing changed, and a colleague whose only sin was cloning on a stock
# Windows laptop watching the suite fail on lines nobody touched. CI never sees
# it: the Linux and macOS jobs check out LF by default and the Windows job pins
# `core.autocrlf=false`.
#
# The same rule for a second reason. `tests/test_park_resume_commands.py`
# asserts that `park`, `resume` and `status` carry the shared estate resolver
# BYTE FOR BYTE — that is what makes the deliberate duplication safe — and a CRLF
# checkout would either break the claim or hide a real drift behind it.
#
# NO PATH IS LISTED BY NAME. Every file this repository ships is text — bash,
# Python, YAML, Markdown, a workflow — so there is nothing for git's binary
# heuristic to misjudge, and `text=auto` leaves that detection where it
# belongs: with git. The pinned `upstream/openRepoShape` is a gitlink and
# carries its own copy of this rule.
#
# openRepoShape #63 wrote this rule for the standard's own root, and its #51
# (2026-09-05) wrote it for every scaffolded project; this is the same rule
# retold for the repository that ships the two commands.

* text=auto eol=lf
